const faq = [
  { q: 'Do Australian Ravens eat lambs?', a: 'Australian Ravens are known to attack weakened or newborn lambs, and this behaviour has historically brought them into serious conflict with sheep farmers in Australia. Research on the extent of this predation suggests that ravens primarily target lambs that are already dead, dying, or severely weakened — healthy lambs are rarely taken. Nevertheless, management programmes were introduced in some states, and the species\' relationship with pastoralism remains a contentious topic in Australian wildlife management.' },
  { q: 'Are Australian Ravens scavengers or hunters?', a: 'Both — but primarily scavengers. The Australian Raven opportunistically takes a wide range of food items, alive and dead. Carrion (roadkill, dead livestock) is an important food source. The species also hunts invertebrates, small reptiles, nestlings, and occasionally small mammals. Its diet mirrors that of other large corvids worldwide: flexible and opportunistic, tilted towards whatever is most abundant and accessible.' },
  { q: 'What do Australian Ravens eat in cities?', a: 'Urban Australian Ravens exploit a wide range of human-associated foods: restaurant and café waste, rubbish bins, roadkill, discarded takeaway food, and the content of outdoor bins. They are intelligent and persistent foragers in urban environments, and their populations in cities like Sydney, Canberra, and Adelaide are partly supported by human food waste.' },
];
