    <h1>Australian Raven Diet: What This Species Eats</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Australian Raven is a highly opportunistic omnivore. Its diet includes carrion (a major component), insects and other invertebrates, small vertebrates (lizards, mice, nestlings), grain and other plant material, eggs, and human food waste. It is one of the most ecologically flexible of the Australian corvids, exploiting a wide range of habitats including cities and farmland.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Primary Food Items</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Based on stomach content analyses and field observations summarised in the <em>Handbook of Australian, New Zealand and Antarctic Birds</em> (HANZAB):<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Carrion:</strong> dead livestock (sheep, cattle), roadkill (kangaroos, wallabies, rabbits, lizards), and other carcasses form a major part of the diet, especially in pastoral and open country.</li>
      <li><strong>Invertebrates:</strong> beetles, grasshoppers, caterpillars, earthworms, and other invertebrates — particularly important in summer when insect abundance is high. Ground-foraging for invertebrates is a significant foraging mode.</li>
      <li><strong>Small vertebrates:</strong> lizards (skinks, geckoes), mice, small birds and nestlings, frogs. Taken opportunistically, not as a dietary staple.</li>
      <li><strong>Grain and seeds:</strong> cereal grains, particularly wheat and oats — eaten from agricultural fields, especially after ploughing and during harvest.</li>
      <li><strong>Eggs:</strong> eggs of ground-nesting birds including quail and plovers.</li>
      <li><strong>Fruit and plant material:</strong> various fruits, berries, and plant material eaten opportunistically.</li>
      <li><strong>Human food waste:</strong> waste from rubbish bins, campsites, urban areas — an increasingly important source in human-modified landscapes.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Conflict with Agriculture</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Australian Raven's interactions with sheep farming have historically been controversial. The species is documented attending lambing and attacking newborn or weakened lambs — pecking at eyes and soft tissue, which can injure or kill stressed animals. Research suggests that most raven attacks on sheep involve already-weak or dying animals rather than healthy ones, but the distinction is difficult to prove in individual cases, and some researchers and farmers report genuine predation on healthy lambs.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>Legal management of Australian Ravens under damage-mitigation permits has been permitted in some states. The species' relationship with agriculture remains an active area of discussion in Australian wildlife management.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Foraging Behaviour</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Australian Ravens forage primarily on the ground — walking steadily and probing for invertebrates, or attending carcasses. They are dominant over smaller corvids (Australian Magpie-Lark, Torresian Crow in some areas) at food sources. Pairs often forage together; outside the breeding season, loose aggregations may form at rich food sources.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

